`[recovered-raw] streamed 8980296 chars to spend the budget` is the line that shows the repair: 8.98 M characters of wire now spends the 8,388,608-character budget, where the old fixture passed 32 MiB without spending it.

**What is real:** the production `createCliJsonlStreamingParser` and the real runner consumer pair `createCliEventHandlers` + `createCliToolTracking`, driven by raw chunk `push()` exactly as `src/agents/cli-runner/execute-process.ts` drives them. The retention figures are forced-collection heap deltas (`setFlagsFromString("--expose-gc")` + two `gc()` passes) and the exported `withExecutionEvidence` projection — no production counter is read, because the removal of those counters is the other half of this commit.

**What is stubbed:** the process edge. No `claude` subprocess runs in this driver; the frames are composed in-process. The real-subprocess-over-a-pipe capture is the sequential-settlement run above (pre-merge head).

**Proof limits, stated plainly:** this is an in-process harness. The review asks for *"a redacted real CLI or Gateway run through `executeCliProcess` that crosses a cumulative budget, continues progress, and delivers its later terminal result"*, and that run **does not exist yet** — neither this driver nor the sequential-settlement capture goes through `executeCliProcess`, so the midstream-cancellation half of the repair is still covered only by mocked-supervisor and in-process tests. `scripts/proof-cli-stream-post-budget-cost.ts` carries the same exhaustion repair but was not re-run here: one full run streams about 1 GiB across ten rounds.

