## What Problem This Solves

Choosing `KIND_EXPERIMENTAL_PROVIDER=docker` did not select Docker in the local
Kind helper. If Podman was responsive, lookup, creation and deletion all used
Podman instead. That can act on the wrong same-named cluster or create an
unexpected second one.

## Changes

Honor Kind's existing provider variable for the helper's supported Docker and
Podman engines. Report an unavailable or unsupported explicit selection instead
of falling back. Keep current auto-detection when the variable is empty. Help
and the Kubernetes guide now show how to use the same selection for creation
and teardown.

## Evidence

Executed the entire shell script on base and fix with isolated command fixtures
for Docker, Podman, Kind and kubectl. Each command and provider was captured;
creation consumes the actual heredoc and reaches the existing readiness command.
No actual daemon or cluster was accessed. macOS 27.0.1 arm64, system Bash 3.2.

Four regressions fail on base and pass with the fix: explicit Docker create,
explicit Docker delete, unavailable explicitly selected Docker, and unsupported
selection. The explicit Podman and both auto-selection controls are unchanged.
All ten tests across the new Kind fixture and existing deployment-teardown suite
passed. Scripts test typecheck, focused lint/formatting, Bash syntax, line-cap
and whitespace checks passed.

