## What Problem This Solves

Gateway SSH routes retained every diagnostic chunk for the whole connection,
although that text is only read when startup fails. A long-lived, noisy SSH
process could therefore keep growing the client's memory after the tunnel was
already usable.

## Changes

Reuse the existing bounded process-output collector for a 64 KiB startup tail,
with a truncation notice when older bytes are omitted. Once the listener has
been admitted, release the captured chunks and continue draining stderr without
retaining it. Startup errors keep their recent diagnostic text; successful
forwarding, cancellation and shutdown keep their existing behavior.

The 64 KiB limit is a diagnostic-retention budget, not a cap that kills an SSH
connection. The UTF-8-safe collector preserves split filenames and the final
unterminated line covered by #141399. No new setting, process runner or transport
owner is introduced.

## Evidence

### Retention through the tunnel owner

Ran the unmodified and fixed `startSshPortForward` with an actual local child,
loopback listener and OS PID ownership inspection. Only the spawned SSH process
was substituted with a synthetic diagnostic producer. It emitted two 16 MiB
batches of unique ASCII after admission. Node 26.12.1, macOS 27.0.1 arm64, forced
GC at each measurement; no external connection or inference.

| Diagnostics emitted after admission | Base retained heap growth | Fix retained heap growth |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| 16 MiB | 16,894,696 bytes | 123,496 bytes |
| 32 MiB | 33,718,288 bytes | 134,080 bytes |

Both versions kept the listener active and drained the pipe to zero buffered
bytes. Both children exited cleanly after `stop()`. These are captured paired
observations, not a statistical performance estimate or a real SSH handshake.

<details>
<summary>Captured results</summary>

```json
{
